.TH READ 2
.SH NAME
read \- read from file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.B int read(int fildes, char *buffer, int nbytes);
.SH DESCRIPTION
A file descriptor is a word
returned from a successful
.I "open, creat, dup,"
or
.I pipe
call.
.I Buffer
is the location of
.I nbytes
contiguous
bytes into which the input will be placed.
It is not guaranteed
that all
.I nbytes
bytes will be read; for example
if the file refers to a typewriter at most one line
will be returned.
In any event the number of characters read is returned.
.PP
If the returned value is 0, then
end-of-file has been reached.
.PP
.B Note:
.B a directory can be read with an ordinary read here,
as it could in v7, and this kernel allows it.  That is what
.IR ttyname (3)
does.  It also means such a program works under the kernel and
.I not
under b6sim, whose
.I read
is the host's and refuses a directory.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
open(2), creat(2), dup(2), pipe(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
As mentioned,
0 is returned when the end of the file has been reached.
If the read was otherwise unsuccessful
the return value is \-1.
Many conditions
can generate an error:
physical I/O errors, bad buffer address,
preposterous
.I nbytes,
file descriptor not that of
an input file.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.B nbytes is a byte count, not a word count,
and one word is
.I six
bytes.  File I/O counts bytes throughout, and a disk block is BSIZE == 3072
bytes == 512 words \- which is what BUFSIZ in <stdio.h> is set from, and why a
page that says 512 bytes is describing a PDP-11.
.PP
.I Buffer
is a fat
.B char *
and the kernel decodes it as one, so an unaligned buffer is not an error.
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_read
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
